#: ../../source/config_making.rst:3
msgid "Making and buying"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:7
msgid "Manufacturing methods"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:9
msgid "The ``manufacturing.method`` field says how a part is made:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:12
msgid "Method"
msgstr "方式"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:12
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:14
msgid "``additive``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:14
msgid "Built up, e.g. 3D printed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:16
msgid "``subtractive``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:16
msgid "Cut away from stock -- see :ref:`subtractive`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:18
msgid "``forming``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:18
msgid "Shaped without adding or removing material"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:20
msgid "``sheet_metal``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:20
msgid "A flat piece bent to shape -- see :ref:`sheet-metal`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:22
msgid "``pcbBasic``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:22
msgid "A printed circuit board (**not implemented yet**)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:25
msgid ""
"These are ways of making a **part**, and apply to parts only. An assembly is "
"put together rather than made, and has a single method of its own -- see :"
"ref:`manufacturing an assembly <assembly-manufacturing>` below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:29
msgid ""
"A part that is bought rather than made carries ``vendor`` and ``sku`` "
"instead of a method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:35
msgid "Subtractive"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:37
msgid ""
"``subtractive`` is the method that takes material away: a router, a laser, a "
"saw, a drill. What it says about a part is not a property of the part's own "
"shape -- almost any solid can be machined out of a big enough block -- but a "
"relation between the part and what it is made *from*, and between the part "
"and the machine that makes it. Both can be declared, and both are checked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:44
msgid "The stock it is cut from"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:46
msgid "``source`` names the piece the part is cut out of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:62
msgid ""
"Cutting can only ever remove material, so the part has to be what is left of "
"the stock. ``pc test`` asks both halves of that, because each catches a "
"different mistake and neither implies the other:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:66
msgid ""
"**Nothing of the part is outside the stock.** A part that pokes out of what "
"it is cut from cannot be made from it however good the machine is -- most "
"often the stock is simply too small, or the part is positioned off it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:69
msgid ""
"**The stock is bigger than the part somewhere.** A part that fills its stock "
"exactly is one whose ``source`` names itself, or a copy of itself, which is "
"the mistake a reader of the YAML cannot see."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:73
msgid ""
"``source`` is **required**, the way :ref:`sheet-metal`'s two fields are, and "
"for the same reason: subtraction is defined by what it starts from. A shape "
"somebody arrived at is not a subtractive part -- what makes it one is that "
"it is what is left of a piece that existed first -- so a declaration naming "
"no stock has not said what the method means."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:79
msgid ""
"A part genuinely made from no stock is a part made some other way: bought "
"(``vendor``/``sku``), ``additive``, or ``forming``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:85
msgid "The machine it is cut on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:87
msgid ""
"Subtraction is one idea, but the machines that do it are not "
"interchangeable, and what they **cannot** do is the useful thing to know. A "
"machine is named by adding its own subsection:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:101
msgid "Subsection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:101
msgid "The machine, and what it cannot do"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:103
msgid "*(none)*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:103
msgid ""
"A CNC router or mill. It follows any 2.5D path, so there is nothing it is "
"held to beyond fitting its stock."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:106
msgid "``cnc:``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:106
msgid "The same machine, said out loud."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:108
msgid "``laser:``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:108
msgid ""
"A laser cutter. Its beam does not tilt, so every wall it makes is parallel "
"to the axis it fires along."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:111
msgid "``drill:``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:111
msgid ""
"A drilling machine. It goes in and comes out, so the only thing it makes is "
"a **round** hole along its own axis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:114
msgid "``cut:``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:114
msgid ""
"A saw that cuts the stock across -- a board to length, a sheet to size. It "
"makes **nothing but the stock with its ends cut off**, where its cuts say. "
"See :ref:`subtractive-cut`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:120
msgid ""
"Naming none of them means CNC. That is the machine that can make anything "
"the other two can, so it is the answer that is never wrong -- and it is what "
"every ``subtractive`` part written before machines could be named already "
"meant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:124
msgid ""
"**Several may be named, and they are alternatives rather than stages.** A "
"part that declares both ``laser:`` and ``cnc:`` is claiming it could be made "
"either way, and ``pc test`` answers for both claims. ``pc cam`` then has to "
"be told which one to write for -- ``pc cam -m laser :gasket`` -- because a "
"default nobody picked is a program for the wrong machine; the file it writes "
"is named after the machine (``gasket.laser.nc``), so the alternatives land "
"beside each other rather than one overwriting the other. A part that really "
"is machined in *stages* is not this: it is a chain of parts, each naming the "
"previous one as its ``source``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:134
msgid ""
"**Only ``subtractive`` has a machine at all**, and the keys that describe a "
"cut are refused everywhere else rather than accepted and never read. A "
"``diameter:`` on a part that says ``method: additive`` is a number somebody "
"chose and nothing acts on, which is the whole reason the job moved out of a "
"section of its own -- a printer has feeds and speeds too, and the day "
"PartCAD writes a program for one they will be a printer's keys under a "
"printer's subsection rather than a router's read by accident. A *sketch* is "
"the one section with no ``method:`` in it: a drawing is not made out of "
"anything, so it names the machine and the job and nothing else."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:144
msgid ""
"Every machine takes a ``toolAxis``, which is the axis the tool, the beam, "
"the drill or the saw approaches along, written as one of ``+X``, ``-X``, "
"``+Y``, ``-Y``, ``+Z`` or ``-Z``. It defaults to ``-Z``: the part sits on "
"the bed and the tool comes down to it. Every machine also takes it as "
"``along:`` -- the same key, the way it is usually said (\"the laser cuts "
"along -Z\") -- and a subsection writes one or the other, never both. It is "
"**not** ``direction:``, which says which way round a contour is cut (climb "
"or conventional) -- the two reach one implementation in one request, which "
"is why they do not share a name. A laser also takes a ``kerf``, the width "
"the beam itself removes, which is a property of that machine and that "
"material rather than of the job -- which is why it is a property of the "
"machine rather than of the cut."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:156 ../../source/config_making.rst:356
msgid "What ``pc test`` checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:158
msgid ""
"Beyond the stock, one question per limited machine, and each applies **only "
"to a part that named that machine**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:161
msgid ""
"``manufacturability-laser`` -- every face of the part is either a wall along "
"the beam or a face across it. A chamfer, a taper, a dome or a fillet rolling "
"over an edge is none of those, and there is no orientation of a beam that "
"produces a surface at an angle to itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:165
msgid ""
"``manufacturability-drill`` -- the same, and every wall is round. This one "
"is asked of what the machine **took away** rather than of the part, where "
"the part names a ``source``: a drilled plate's straight sides came with the "
"stock, and asking the part's own walls would fail every plate for having "
"them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:169
msgid ""
"``manufacturability-cut`` -- the stock, cut where every declared cut says, "
"**is** the part. See :ref:`subtractive-cut`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:172
msgid ""
"Both are measured by sampling each face's own normal against the axis, not "
"by reading its surface type. The type is not the question: a cylinder is a "
"wall when it is coaxial with the axis and a defect when it lies across it, "
"and a surface extruded along the axis is a perfectly good wall whatever it "
"is made of."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:177
msgid ""
"What is deliberately **not** checked is thickness, material or power. A "
"laser will cut a 20 mm plate as readily as a 1 mm sheet given enough of it, "
"and how much is enough is a property of the machine and the material rather "
"than of the design -- so it is not something PartCAD can answer from the "
"geometry, and a check that guessed would refuse parts the shop next door "
"cuts every day."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:184
msgid "What ``pc cam`` writes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:186
msgid ""
"The same ``gcode`` file type produces a different program for each machine, "
"and which one it writes for is the part's own statement rather than a job "
"parameter -- what a part is made on is a property of the part, and a re-"
"tunable key for it would be a route written for a machine nobody owns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:192
msgid "Machine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:192
msgid "The program"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:194
msgid "CNC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:194
msgid ""
"Contours offset by the cutter's radius, cut at stepped depths. ``M3``/``M5`` "
"where a ``speed`` is named."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:197
msgid "Laser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:197
msgid ""
"One pass, offset by half the ``kerf``, with the beam gated ``M3 S<power>``/"
"``M5`` around each contour and no Z motion at all. Reads no ``tool`` and no "
"``depth_per_pass``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:201
msgid "Drilling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:201
msgid ""
"A rapid to each round feature's centre, a plunge and a retract, broken into "
"steps where ``peck:`` says."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:205
msgid ""
"A ``cut:`` gets no program: a saw cutting stock to size is told where to cut "
"and nothing else, and the part's declaration already says that. ``pc cam`` "
"passes over a part that is only cut, and refuses ``-m cut``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:209
msgid ""
"``power`` (a laser's S-word) and ``peck`` (how deep a drill goes before "
"clearing the swarf) join the other :ref:`cam job keys <cam-section>`. A part "
"declaring a ``toolAxis`` other than ``-Z`` is rotated into the machine's "
"frame before the route is written, so the program is in the coordinates the "
"part is fixtured in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:214
msgid ""
"``examples/produce_part_subtractive`` is the whole of the above in one "
"package: two stocks, a laser-cut blank and gasket, a routed block whose "
"chamfer is the one feature only a router can make, a drilled plate, and a "
"rail cut to length off a board. The blank is what :ref:`sheet-metal` bends. "
"The desk in ``//pub/furniture/workspace/basic`` is cut, every piece of it, "
"with ``cut:``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:223
msgid "Cutting stock to size"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:225
msgid ""
"``cut:`` is the machine most parts that are cut at all are cut on: a saw "
"taking a stud off an eight foot 2x4, or a shelf out of a sheet of plywood. "
"It follows no outline, so it is described by **where** it cuts, in the "
"part's own coordinates, which are also its stock's:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:251
msgid ""
"A saw is written the way every other machine is: by its ``toolAxis``, which "
"points at the **offcut**. Each cut starts where the stock starts along that "
"axis -- the way a board is measured from its end -- travels ``length:`` into "
"it, and cuts across; everything further along the axis is cut off, and what "
"is behind the saw is the part. So ``toolAxis: -Y`` counts from the other end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:257
msgid "``length:`` is always there: it is the whole of *where*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:258
msgid ""
"``toolAxis:`` on a cut, or ``along:`` -- which says the same, the way a saw "
"cut is usually said; one or the other -- is the axis that cut travels along, "
"as an axis (``+Y``) or a vector (``[0, 1, 0]``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:261
msgid ""
"A cut that names neither travels along the machine's ``toolAxis:`` (or "
"``along:``), and a machine that names none has the default every machine "
"has, ``-Z``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:265
msgid ""
"Any number in a cut may be written as ``$name`` -- the value of the part's "
"own parameter of that name, with a unit after it if it needs one (``$length "
"in``). A part cut to length is nearly always parametric, and a cut written "
"as a number would be right for one instance and wrong for every other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:270
msgid ""
"``pc test`` runs ``manufacturability-cut`` over it, which makes each cut in "
"the stock in turn and compares what is left with the part. Whatever the part "
"has that the cut stock does not is a feature no saw made; whatever the cut "
"stock has that the part does not is a notch, a hole or a cut in the wrong "
"place; and a cut that takes nothing off the stock is one that misses it -- "
"usually a length longer than the board, or an axis pointing the wrong way. "
"Each of the three is a failure that says which plane it was about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:281
msgid "Sheet metal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:283
msgid ""
"``sheet_metal`` is the one method that is not described by the part alone. "
"The others say how a shape is produced from stock; this one says that an "
"existing flat piece was put through a brake, so it names two things instead:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:298
msgid ""
"``source`` -- **required.** The part that goes into the brake: the flat "
"blank. It is a reference, resolved against this package like every other "
"reference a part makes, and it points at a **part** rather than at a drawing "
"because it is one. It has a thickness, a material, a tolerance, and a "
"manufacturing method of its own."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:304
msgid ""
"``instructions`` -- **required.** The sketch that says where the bends are "
"and what each of them is. It is a reference to a :ref:`sketch <sketches>`, "
"so it may carry parameters: a drawing that holds the outline *and* the bend "
"lines is read as bends alone with ``;include=BEND_UP,BEND_DOWN``, which is "
"what the DXF layer parameters are for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:311
msgid "The outline belongs to the blank"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:313
msgid ""
"The outline of the part, its holes, its slots and its cut-outs are **not** "
"the sheet metal process's to make, and must not be described as part of it. "
"They belong to the blank, which is usually cut flat -- laser, waterjet, "
"punch, router -- and so is usually an ordinary ``subtractive`` part:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:341
msgid ""
"That is how it is made, and so it is how it is written down. A shop cuts the "
"flat pattern on one machine and bends it on another, they are quoted, "
"toleranced and scheduled separately, and the flat pattern is a thing that "
"exists -- it is what arrives at the brake. Describing a hole as part of the "
"bending step would put it on the process that cannot make it, and would "
"leave the part with no declaration of the process that can."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:348
msgid ""
"``subtractive`` is the usual answer rather than a required one: what has to "
"be true of a ``source`` is that it is flat, which is what ``pc test`` asks "
"of it. A blank that is bought in rather than made has no manufacturing "
"method to declare, one sheared or punched to outline is ``forming``, and an "
"``alias`` or an ``enrich`` of a part declared elsewhere carries whatever "
"that one says. All of them are blanks, and the check takes them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:358
msgid "Two questions, one about each half of the declaration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:360
msgid ""
"**The blank is flat, top and bottom.** What goes into a brake is a piece of "
"sheet, so the horizontal plane through its highest point and the one through "
"its lowest each meet it in an area rather than touching it at a point. A "
"``source`` that fails this is not a blank -- most often it is the bent part "
"itself, named by mistake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:366
msgid ""
"**Every bend line says what kind of bend it is.** Each line of the "
"instructions sketch carries three annotations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:370
msgid "Annotation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:370
msgid "What it has to be"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:372
msgid "``angle``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:372
msgid "How far the metal is turned, in degrees. Positive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:374
msgid "``radius``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:374
msgid ""
"The **inner** radius of the bend, in millimetres. More than zero -- zero is "
"a fold, not a bend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:377
msgid "``direction``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:377
msgid "``up`` or ``down``, in either case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:380
msgid ""
"Where they come from is the sketch's business, not this check's: a DXF "
"states them as :ref:`extended data <sketch-annotations>`, and the check "
"reads what the sketch reports rather than the file it was read from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:384
msgid ""
"What is *not* checked is the geometry of the part against those "
"instructions. Whether bending the blank as described produces the shape the "
"part declares is a question for the day PartCAD bends the blank itself; "
"until then the part is what its own type built, and these are the "
"manufacturing inputs beside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:389
msgid ""
"``examples/produce_part_sheet_metal`` is the whole of the above in one "
"package: one DXF holding the flat pattern and two bend lines, a blank "
"extruded from the outline layer, and three parts folded from that one blank "
"-- at one bend line, at the other, and at both. They differ in nothing but "
"which layers their ``instructions`` select, which is the case the layer "
"parameters exist for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:399
msgid "Procurement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:401
msgid ""
"A part that can be bought off the shelf instead of being manufactured is "
"declared using the following syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:414
msgid "``vendor``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:416
msgid "Optional. The vendor that sells the part."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:418
msgid "``sku``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:420
msgid ""
"Optional. The vendor's `stock keeping unit <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Stock_keeping_unit>`_ identifying what is ordered from that vendor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:424
msgid ""
"Both ``vendor`` and ``sku`` must be set for the part to be considered "
"purchasable. If either is missing, the part has to be manufactured instead, "
"which relies on the MCFTT parameters described above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:428
msgid "``count_per_sku``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:430
msgid "Optional. Defaults to ``1``. Must be a positive integer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:432
msgid ""
"The number of parts that come in a single SKU, for the parts that are sold "
"in packs: a bag of 25 nuts is one SKU that yields 25 parts. Providers use it "
"to translate the number of parts requested into the number of SKUs to order, "
"and the number of SKUs a store has in stock into the number of parts it can "
"supply."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:438
msgid "``item_in_sku``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:440
msgid ""
"Optional. Which item this is, where one SKU is a *set* of different things "
"rather than a pack of one: a shaft sold with the clip that goes on it, a "
"bracket that comes with its screws. Each of them is an object of its own -- "
"used on its own, assembled on its own, possibly taken apart and put back "
"later -- so each is declared as a part of its own. Each names the set's "
"``vendor`` and ``sku``, and says which item of the set it is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:461
msgid ""
"``count_per_sku`` is then how many of that one kind come in a set. What is "
"ordered is the set, as many times as the most demanding kind needs: two "
"shafts and two clips are two sets, not four. Objects of one SKU that do not "
"name an ``item_in_sku`` are one kind with each other, which is what a SKU of "
"one kind of thing is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:467
msgid ""
"An object sold in more than one set -- the same clip comes with every length "
"of shaft -- is declared once for the geometry, with an ``alias`` of it for "
"each set it comes in, the way a part sold by several vendors is (below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:471
msgid ""
"These values are passed on to providers of the type ``store`` as "
"``request[\"vendor\"]``, ``request[\"sku\"]``, "
"``request[\"count_per_sku\"]`` and ``request[\"item_in_sku\"]`` (see :ref:"
"`providers`). A cart also says what to order, one line per SKU, in "
"``request[\"cart\"][\"skus\"]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:479
msgid "Parts that are made"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:481
msgid ""
"A part with manufacturing instructions -- a ``manufacturing:`` section "
"naming a method -- is **made**, and whoever builds the assembly is taken at "
"their word that they can make it: PartCAD has the instructions, and for now "
"assumes the capabilities to follow them. So nobody is asked to supply such a "
"part. What has to be procured is what it is made *from*: the ``source:`` of "
"its ``manufacturing:`` section, which is procured by the same rule in turn "
"-- a bracket bent from a blank that is cut from a sheet is procured as the "
"sheet. A part made from nothing it names (``additive``, ``forming``) needs "
"nothing procured at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:491
msgid ""
"**The bills of materials** list every part that goes into the assembly, and "
"beside them a **Stock** section: what the made parts are made from, one "
"piece for each part made from it, and which parts each is for. Cutting "
"several parts out of one piece is a question of layout that PartCAD does not "
"answer yet, so the count is what buying for each part separately would take "
"-- an upper bound, never short. The detailed bill of materials (``pc bom``) "
"lists what has to be procured: a made part is not a line item of its own "
"there but the stock it is made from, of kind ``stock``, with the vendor and "
"the SKU to order it by."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:500
msgid ""
"**The assembly instructions** open, after the bill of materials, with the "
"parts to manufacture: each one, how many of it, what it is made from, and "
"its manufacturing instructions written out in full."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:503
msgid "**pc supply** puts the stock in the cart instead of the part."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:504
msgid ""
"**pc test** does not look for a supplier of a made part. It checks that its "
"instructions are complete (a tolerance), and that its stock can be had -- by "
"running the same tests over the stock, which for a bought one is a supplier "
"that carries it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:509
msgid ""
"A part that has manufacturing instructions **and** a ``vendor`` and an "
"``sku`` can be had either way. It is tried as bought first: ``pc test`` asks "
"for a supplier, and only if no supplier confirms it does it check that the "
"part can be made instead. The bills of materials and the cart list it as "
"bought."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:514
msgid ""
"Note that ``count_per_sku`` is a property of how the part is packaged for "
"sale, not of the CAD model. If the same part is sold by several vendors in "
"different pack sizes, declare one part per (vendor, SKU) pair, for example "
"using ``alias``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:537
msgid ""
"What an object is bought as is the one thing an ``alias`` or an ``enrich`` "
"may state of its own: everything else it reports -- where the file is, what "
"it is built with, which parameter values it has -- belongs to the "
"declaration it resolves to. A reference that names a ``vendor`` or an "
"``sku`` replaces the whole record, ``count_per_sku`` included, because a "
"pack size written for one SKU says nothing about another one; an absent "
"``count_per_sku`` therefore reads as ``1``, the same as it would on a part "
"declared outright. A reference that names neither is ordering the same "
"thing, so the record stands -- and a ``count_per_sku`` on its own corrects "
"how many of that same SKU arrive in one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:547
msgid ""
"This travels down a chain: an alias of an alias, and an enrich of an enrich, "
"report what the reference in the middle declared rather than only what is at "
"the end of the chain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:555
msgid "Materials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:557
msgid ""
"A part is made of something, and ``materials`` is where a package says what "
"that something is. It is the object the ``material`` parameter of a part "
"points at (see :ref:`parameters`), so that naming a substance is naming a "
"thing PartCAD can ask questions of rather than repeating a string every "
"reader has to interpret for themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:563
msgid ""
"A material is **not** a shape. PLA has no geometry: there is nothing to "
"render, to export or to tessellate, and none of what :ref:`parts` and :ref:"
"`assemblies` can do applies to it. What it is, is a set of facts about a "
"substance:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:579
msgid "The short form gives the full name and nothing else:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:586
msgid ""
"Density is in ``g/mm^3``, the units every length in PartCAD is already in, "
"so that a mass falls out of a volume without a conversion nobody remembers "
"to apply. Datasheets quote ``g/cm^3``, which is 1000 times larger: PLA at "
"1.32 g/cm^3 is declared as ``0.00132``. A material that states no density "
"reports no mass, rather than a mass of zero -- nothing downstream could tell "
"an invented figure apart from a stated one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:593
msgid ""
"``tags`` is free-form on purpose. There is no controlled vocabulary of "
"material properties that survives contact with real catalogues, and imposing "
"one would only mean packages could not say what they mean."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:597
msgid ""
"Materials are addressed like every other object, as ``<package>:<name>``, so "
"a part in one package names a material catalogued in another:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:610
msgid ""
"List what a package catalogues with ``pc list materials`` (and ``pc list "
"materials //...`` to walk the packages it imports)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:614
msgid "Standard catalogues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:616
msgid ""
"The PartCAD index publishes two families of standard materials, so that a "
"part made of something ordinary need not restate any of the above:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:619
msgid ""
"``//pub/std/manufacturing/material/plastic`` -- polymers: the commodity and "
"printable thermoplastics (``pla``, ``abs``, ``asa``, ``petg``, ``pc``), the "
"engineering ones (``nylon``, ``pa12``, ``pa66-gf30``, ``pom``, ``uhmwpe``), "
"the high-performance ones (``ptfe``, ``peek``, ``pei``, ``pps``), the "
"polymer-matrix composites (``cfrp``, ``gfrp``) and the elastomers (``tpu``, "
"``nr``, ``nbr``, ``epdm``, ``fkm``, ``silicone``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:626
msgid ""
"``//pub/std/manufacturing/material/metal`` -- metals and alloys, named by "
"their standard designation and temper: ``al-5052-h32``, ``al-6061-t6``, "
"``al-7075-t6``, ``steel-4130``, ``ss-316l``, ``ti-6al-4v``, ``inconel-718`` "
"and the rest of what robotics, aviation and automotive parts are made of."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:631
msgid ""
"An alloy is named ``<metal>-<designation>-<temper>`` rather than by the bare "
"designation for two reasons: ``5052:`` in YAML is the *number* 5052 rather "
"than a name, and a temper is part of what was ordered -- 6061-T6 and 6061-O "
"are one alloy and not one material to build out of."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:640
msgid "Software"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:642
msgid ""
"A product is rarely hardware alone: the board in it runs a firmware image, "
"the controller boots a disk image, the tool that talks to it is a binary on "
"the host. ``software`` declares those as objects of the package, beside its "
"parts and its assemblies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:647
msgid ""
"Software is **not** a shape. There is no geometry to render, to export or to "
"measure, and none of what :ref:`parts` and :ref:`assemblies` can do applies "
"to it. What it is, always, is a *file*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:664
msgid "The short form declares nothing but the path:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:671
msgid ""
"``path`` behaves as it does everywhere else (see :ref:`files`): without it "
"the file is the object's own name, and a file the package does not carry is "
"declared with ``fileFrom``/``fileUrl`` and fetched lazily. The default path "
"carries no extension, because a firmware image is as likely to be a ``."
"img``, a ``.uf2`` or nothing at all as it is a ``.bin``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:677
msgid ""
"``raw`` is the file handed over as it is: PartCAD carries it, says which one "
"it is, and what to do with it is the reader's business. Every type is a file "
"and that will not change -- the types that come after ``raw`` name the "
"*procedure* the file goes through rather than a different kind of object, "
"associating a specific firmware flashing procedure (which tool, which "
"bootloader, which reset dance) with the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:685
msgid "Which software an object ships with"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:687
msgid ""
"A part or an assembly says what it ships with in its own ``software`` list. "
"A bare name is software of the same package; a qualified one is software of "
"another:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:707
msgid ""
"``software`` is optional, and most parts declare none. A single one may be "
"written on its own instead of as a list (``software: controller-firmware``). "
"Declaring it is what puts the file into the bill of materials of every "
"assembly the part ends up in, and what makes ``pc test`` insist the file be "
"obtainable (see `Manufacturability`_ below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:713
msgid ""
"The reference is resolved against the package that *wrote* it, so an "
"``alias`` or an ``enrich`` of that part in another package still points at "
"the same file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:717
msgid "In the bill of materials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:719
msgid ""
"Every assembly's bill of materials lists the software of the parts and sub-"
"assemblies it is made of, and its own, under a heading of its own:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:733
msgid ""
"Each software line names the package it came from **and the revision of that "
"package** -- the commit its files were read at. A bracket is the same "
"bracket whenever it is fetched; a firmware image is a different file as soon "
"as its package publishes again, so the revision is what makes the line mean "
"something. A package that is not in a git repository has no revision, and "
"the line says so rather than inventing one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:740
msgid ""
"The count is how many times something in the assembly needs it: three boards "
"running one image is a count of three, the same way three of anything else "
"is. A sub-assembly that is bought whole -- it declares a vendor and an SKU, "
"and a supplier has it available -- is not expanded, so its firmware is no "
"more a line item than its screws are."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:746
msgid ""
"Software is not procured: ``pc supply`` and the manufacturability tests walk "
"the hardware only, because nobody sells a firmware image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:750
msgid "In the package's README"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:752
msgid ""
"``pc render -t readme`` lists the software of a package in a table of its "
"own, saying which file each one is, the version it declares, and the hash it "
"is pinned to. The file is linked where the package carries it; where it is "
"fetched, the URL it comes from is shown instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:758
msgid "Which file is it?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:760
msgid ""
"The whole point of listing software beside the hardware is being able to say "
"which file went into a product. There are two ways a package can be that "
"specific, and ``pc lint`` requires one of them (the ``Software`` check):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:764
msgid ""
"The package **carries the file**. It is content of the repository, so the "
"revision recorded beside every software line item identifies it exactly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:766
msgid ""
"The package **pulls it in** with ``fileFrom``, and pins it with "
"``fileHash``. Without a hash nothing identifies it: the URL serves whatever "
"it serves at the moment it is fetched, and the same package revision "
"produces a different image tomorrow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:784
msgid ""
"``fileHash`` is not a software-specific idea: it pins the bytes of any file "
"a package fetches rather than carries, and the download is refused unless "
"they match (see :ref:`file-hash` for the spelling and the details). "
"Everywhere it is required for the object to be manufacturable; software is "
"the one kind where a missing one is reported by ``pc lint`` as well, before "
"anything is built."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:790
msgid ""
"See ``examples/produce_software`` for a package that does both, and "
"``examples/produce_part_kicad`` for a board that pulls its host-side tool "
"from a public URL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:795
msgid "Manufacturability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:797
msgid ""
"A board nobody can flash is not a board anybody can make. So the "
"manufacturing test (``pc test``, the ``manufacturability`` check) asks the "
"same question of a part's ``software`` that it asks of everything else the "
"part needs, and the part fails unless all of it holds:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:802
msgid "every reference resolves to a software object;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:803
msgid "the file is there -- carried by the package, or fetched successfully;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:804
msgid "it matches its ``fileHash``, where one is declared;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:805
msgid ""
"and the declaration is reproducible at all, which is the same ``fileFrom``-"
"needs-a-``fileHash`` rule the part's own file is held to (:ref:"
"`reproducibility`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:809
msgid ""
"That applies whether the part is bought or made: buying the board does not "
"answer the question of which image goes on it. An assembly that declares "
"software of its own is held to the same rule; its parts' software is checked "
"by their own run of the test."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:814
msgid ""
"Software is otherwise absent from procurement -- ``pc supply`` walks the "
"hardware only, because nobody sells a firmware image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:821
msgid "Providers"
msgstr "提供方"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:823
msgid "Providers are declared in ``partcad.yaml`` using the following syntax:"
msgstr "提供方在 ``partcad.yaml`` 中使用以下语法声明："

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:844
msgid ""
"``enrich`` providers are just references to other providers with some "
"parameters modified to specific values. ``with:`` sets any of the parameters "
"the source provider declares, the same way an ``enrich`` part's ``with:`` "
"does, and under the same rule: a value may not contain ``,``, ``;`` or "
"``=``. ``currency`` is also read by PartCAD itself, as what the provider's "
"quotes are in, so it has to be a name such as ``USD``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:851
msgid ""
"``store`` and ``manufacturer`` providers are implemented as Python scripts. "
"These scripts are invoked using the ``runpy`` module which allows to pass "
"input as values of global objects. The outputs are also extracted from the "
"value of global objects."
msgstr ""
"``store`` 和 ``manufacturer`` 提供方以 Python 脚本的形式实现。这些脚本使用 "
"``runpy`` 模块来调用，该模块允许以全局对象的值的形式传入输入。输出也是从全局"
"对象的值中提取的。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:856
msgid ""
"The input is passed as the dictionary ``request``. The output is extracted "
"from the dictionary ``output``"
msgstr "输入以字典 ``request`` 的形式传入。输出则从字典 ``output`` 中提取"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:860
msgid "Store"
msgstr "Store"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:862
msgid "``store`` providers use the following input and output values:"
msgstr "``store`` 提供方使用以下输入和输出值："

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:864 ../../source/config_making.rst:922
msgid ""
"`request[\"parameters\"]`: The configuration parameters of the provider."
msgstr "`request[\"parameters\"]`：提供方的配置参数。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:865 ../../source/config_making.rst:923
msgid "`request[\"api\"]`: The API method called."
msgstr "`request[\"api\"]`：被调用的 API 方法。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:867 ../../source/config_making.rst:925
msgid "`request[\"api\"] == \"caps\"`"
msgstr "`request[\"api\"] == \"caps\"`"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:869
msgid ""
"Get capabilities of this provider. Currently PartCAD does not use "
"capabilities for ``store`` providers."
msgstr "获取该提供方的能力。目前 PartCAD 不会为 ``store`` 提供方使用能力信息。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:872
msgid "`output`: no output is expected"
msgstr "`output`：不期望有任何输出"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:874
msgid "`request[\"api\"] == \"avail\"`"
msgstr "`request[\"api\"] == \"avail\"`"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:876
msgid "Check availability of the specific part."
msgstr "检查特定零件的可用性。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:878
msgid "`request[\"vendor\"]`: the vendor of the part"
msgstr "`request[\"vendor\"]`：该零件的供应商"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:879
msgid "`request[\"sku\"]`: the SKU of the part"
msgstr "`request[\"sku\"]`：该零件的 SKU"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:880
msgid "`request[\"count\"]`: the requested quantity of the parts"
msgstr "`request[\"count\"]`：所请求的零件数量"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:881
msgid "`request[\"count_per_sku\"]`: the known number of parts per SKU"
msgstr "`request[\"count_per_sku\"]`：每个 SKU 已知包含的零件数量"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:882
msgid ""
"`request[\"item_in_sku\"]`: which item of the SKU this is, where the SKU is "
"a set of several kinds of items, or `None`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:884
msgid "`output[\"available\"]`: boolean, whether it is available in this store"
msgstr "`output[\"available\"]`：布尔值，表示该零件在此商店中是否可用"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:886 ../../source/config_making.rst:941
msgid "`request[\"api\"] == \"quote\"`"
msgstr "`request[\"api\"] == \"quote\"`"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:888 ../../source/config_making.rst:943
msgid ""
"Get a quote for the specific cart of parts. Quote API is the core of the "
"provider. It is expected to return the price of a cart."
msgstr ""
"为特定的零件购物车获取报价。报价 API 是提供方的核心，它应当返回一个购物车的价"
"格。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:892 ../../source/config_making.rst:947
msgid "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"]`: the dictionary of parts"
msgstr "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"]`：零件的字典"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:893
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"vendor\"]`: the vendor of the part"
msgstr "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"vendor\"]`：该零件的供应商"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:894
msgid "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"sku\"]`: the SKU of the part"
msgstr "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"sku\"]`：该零件的 SKU"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:895
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"count\"]`: the requested quantity of "
"the parts"
msgstr "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"count\"]`：所请求的零件数量"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:896
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"count_per_sku\"]`: the known number of "
"parts per SKU"
msgstr ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"count_per_sku\"]`：每个 SKU 已知包含的"
"零件数量"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:897
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"item_in_sku\"]`: which item of the SKU "
"the part is, where the SKU is a set of several kinds of items (absent "
"otherwise)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:899
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"skus\"]`: what to order, a list with one entry per SKU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:900
msgid "`request[\"cart\"][\"skus\"][<n>][\"vendor\"]`, `[\"sku\"]`: the SKU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:901
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"skus\"][<n>][\"count\"]`: how many of that SKU to "
"order, which is already worked out from `count_per_sku` and `item_in_sku`. "
"Order from here rather than once per part: a SKU that is a set of several "
"parts would otherwise be bought once for each of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:905
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"skus\"][<n>][\"parts\"]`: the parts that SKU is for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:906 ../../source/config_making.rst:950
msgid "`output[\"price\"]`: the total price of the cart"
msgstr "`output[\"price\"]`：购物车的总价"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:907 ../../source/config_making.rst:951
msgid ""
"`output[\"cartId\"]`: the id of the cart (to be used for the order later)"
msgstr "`output[\"cartId\"]`：购物车的 id（稍后用于下单）"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:909 ../../source/config_making.rst:953
msgid "`request[\"api\"] == \"order\"`"
msgstr "`request[\"api\"] == \"order\"`"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:911 ../../source/config_making.rst:955
msgid ""
"Order the specific quote. Order API does not need to be implemented as there "
"is no infrastructure for payments yet."
msgstr ""
"对特定的报价下单。由于目前还没有支付相关的基础设施，因此下单 API 无需实现。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:915 ../../source/config_making.rst:959
msgid "`request[\"cartId\"]`: the id of the cart to be purchased"
msgstr "`request[\"cartId\"]`：要购买的购物车的 id"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:918
msgid "Manufacturer"
msgstr "Manufacturer"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:920
msgid "``manufacturer`` providers use the following input and output values:"
msgstr "``manufacturer`` 提供方使用以下输入和输出值："

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:927
msgid "Get capabilities of this provider."
msgstr "获取该提供方的能力。"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:929
msgid "`output[\"materials\"]`: the dictionary of supported materials"
msgstr "`output[\"materials\"]`：所支持材料的字典"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:939
msgid "`output[\"format\"]`: the list of supported formats (e.g. `[\"step\"]`)"
msgstr "`output[\"format\"]`：所支持格式的列表（例如 `[\"step\"]` ）"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:948
msgid ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"format\"]`: the format of the binary "
"(e.g. `\"step\"`)"
msgstr ""
"`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"format\"]`：二进制数据的格式（例如 "
"`\"step\"` ）"

#: ../../source/config_making.rst:949
msgid "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"binary\"]`: the geometry data"
msgstr "`request[\"cart\"][\"parts\"][<id>][\"binary\"]`：几何数据"
